{
  "id": "ep075",
  "duration": 53.034,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.018,
      "text": "The Moon's brightest rocks are too young to exist.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Back side of the Moon AS16-3021.jpg",
        "credit": "Apollo 16 astronauts",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Back_side_of_the_Moon_AS16-3021.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.018,
      "end": 10.688,
      "text": "Apollo astronauts brought back samples of anorthosite, a highly reflective, coarse-grained rock covering the lunar highlands.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "AS15-86-11687 (21495902168).jpg",
        "credit": "Project Apollo Archive",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AS15-86-11687_(21495902168).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.033,
          "end": 6.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 6.867,
          "end": 10.7,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.688,
      "end": 17.833,
      "text": "Scientists believed the entire Moon once had a global magma ocean, where these light crystals floated to the top.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "AS15-86-11687 (21495902168).jpg",
        "credit": "Project Apollo Archive",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AS15-86-11687_(21495902168).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.7,
          "end": 17.833,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.833,
      "end": 25.128,
      "text": "To study this primordial crust, geologists compared it to similar rock deposits in Canada and Scandinavia.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Marcy Anorthosite (Mesoproterozoic, 1.16 Ga; Route 3 roadcut southwest of Saranac Lake, New York State, USA) 60.jpg",
        "credit": "James St. John",
        "license": "CC BY 2.0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marcy_Anorthosite_(Mesoproterozoic,_1.16_Ga;_Route_3_roadcut_southwest_of_Saranac_Lake,_New_York_State,_USA)_60.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.833,
          "end": 21.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 21.467,
          "end": 25.133,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 25.128,
      "end": 33.223,
      "text": "But a 2011 study re-dated lunar Sample 60025 to just 4.36 billion years old.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 25.133,
          "end": 33.233,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 33.223,
      "end": 39.048,
      "text": "This young age shattered the theory of a single, rapidly cooling global magma ocean.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "designed card"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 33.233,
          "end": 39.033,
          "kind": "designed",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 39.048,
      "end": 46.468,
      "text": "Now, scientists are debating whether the Moon's crust actually built up slowly through multiple volcanic episodes.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Igneous rock eng modified.jpg",
        "credit": "The Editor's Apprentice",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Igneous_rock_eng_modified.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 39.033,
          "end": 46.467,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 46.468,
      "end": 52.684,
      "text": "To solve how planets form, we first have to explain why the Moon's brightest rocks are too young to exist.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Back side of the Moon AS16-3021.jpg",
        "credit": "Apollo 16 astronauts",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Back_side_of_the_Moon_AS16-3021.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 46.467,
          "end": 53.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
